// expect: compile_error
// expect-error: E476
// Expected: E476 Ambiguous Type Variable
//   "Type argument 2 of `Convert` is not determined by this signature, so no
//    call can select an instance for it."
//   Hint: "A constrained type variable must appear in the signature's own
//    type, otherwise no call can determine which instance to use."
//
// Proposal 0179 Stage 4, Haskell Report 4.3.4: `describe` constrains
// `Convert<a, b>`, but `b` appears nowhere in `(a) -> Int`. Instantiating the
// signature at any call site leaves `b` free, so no call can ever select an
// instance for it — the signature is unusable as written, independently of
// which instances exist.
//
// Deferred from Stage 3, which could not express this: `where` bounds emitted
// only the type parameter they were attached to, so `where Convert<a, b>`
// became the arity-1 predicate `Convert<a>` and `b` was not merely
// undetermined — it was absent.
//
// Distinct from E459, which reports one *call* that leaves a parameter open.
// This is a property of the signature, so every call to it is affected.

class Convert<a, b> {
    fn convert(x: a) -> b
}

instance Convert<Int, String> {
    fn convert(x) { to_string(x) }
}

fn describe<a, b>(x: a) -> Int where Convert<a, b> {
    1
}

fn main() with IO {
    print(describe(42))
}
